-point/questions ["question-a80e40f5-874e-56ab-80fe-9d43a174a171" "question-68db7057-3186-55cb-a475-e68617c8e05d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179423 :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)$ is $-3\\lt x \\leq 3$ and the range of $f$ is $-2\\leq f(x) \\lt 2.$ What is the range of the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-3\\lt x \\leq 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-3\\lt  f^{-1}(x) \\leq 3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-177999f4-8826-5bd4-8370-aadea8a26d3f" :question/id #uuid "177999f4-8826-5bd4-8370-aadea8a26d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-155964" :question/problem "![](images/42/4297165a04f0e68c0f1d1b59d39643734e11bcd1809bf1946e4c3b67bee3a601.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ shown above, what is the domain of the inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the range of $f(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n-1<f(x)\\le 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n-1<x\\le 3\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3080165f-6478-50dd-b12c-9ef47d4dc9ce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3080165f-6478-50dd-b12c-9ef47d4dc9ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "3080165f-6478-50dd-b12c-9ef47d4dc9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-667a998f-6463-5da3-8065-7e6a8ea8a6ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a04b075d-3871-5cba-afd7-8a83a53b115b" "answ