f}^{-1}(x)<4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a360d025-c375-5503-9d95-becd0af97805" :answer/id #uuid "a360d025-c375-5503-9d95-becd0af97805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\le {f}^{-1}(x)\\le 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccbb1c10-5851-56e1-8bd3-1e66ddc4a54f" :answer/id #uuid "ccbb1c10-5851-56e1-8bd3-1e66ddc4a54f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26720517-4d72-5460-b56e-b19365511cf0" :answer/id #uuid "26720517-4d72-5460-b56e-b19365511cf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-68db7057-3186-55cb-a475-e68617c8e05d" :question/id #uuid "68db7057-3186-55cb-a475-e68617c8e05d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108477" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)$ is $-1\\le x\\le 9$ and the range of $f$ is $-2\\le f(x)\\le 11.$ What is the domain of the inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nThe range of $f(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n-2\\le f(x)\\le 11\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n-2\\le x\\le 11\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc2e91fa-233b-5f55-9b5d-8a3d23d9b72f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc2e91fa-233b-5f55-9b5d-8a3d23d9b72f" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc2e91fa-233b-5f55-9b5d-8a3d23d9b72f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f9c684f0-2711-5edf-8cff-d6913f105ea3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e12067e2-5fe8-5678-823b-e88909af3615" "answer-2a8403c3-9430-52e7-946d-dcfca1e36081" "answer-3dd83ed2-4a6b-5d6c-bbc0-0f8245736c3a" "answer-f9c684f0-2711-5edf-8cff-d6913f105ea3" "answer-70b3347f-c4e0-5cbf-b7bb-0f2bc1f18395"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e12067e2-5fe8-5678-823b-e88909af3615" :answer/id #uuid "e12067e2-5fe8-5678-823b-e88909af3615" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a8403c3-9430-52e7-946d-dcfca1e36081" :answer/id #uuid "2a8403c3-9430-52e7-946d-dcfca1e36081" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3dd83ed2-4a6b-5d6c-bbc0-0f8245736c3a" :answer/id #uuid "3dd83ed2-4a6b-5d6c-bbc0-0f8245736c3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le -1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9c684f0-2711-5edf-8cff-d6913f105ea3" :answer/id #uuid "f9c684f0-2711-5edf-8cff-d6913f105ea3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70b3347f-c4e0-5cbf-b7bb-0f2bc1f18395" :answer/id #uuid "70b3347f-c4e0-5cbf-b7bb-0f2bc1f18395" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180753 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/c0/c0bd305d326688f49ecf0579ee42217a58a7521be8a410f619cbe26f5c443991.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n- the domain of $f(x)$ is $[1,2],$ and\n\n- the range of $f(x)$ is $[2,6].$\n\nRecall that each point $(a,b)$ on the graph of ${\\color{blue}y=f(x)}$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on the graph of the inverse function ${\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}.$ That is to say, the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap.\n\n![](images/1d/1dd83a834e14f8f3679db1c013f23cce80ec9b2546393cf77ee613140c6b7b5f.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the domain of $f^{-1}(x)$ is $[2,6],$ and\n\n- the range of $f^{-1}(x)$ is $[1,2].$\n\nIn general:\n\n- The domain of a function ${\\color{blue}f(x)}$ is the range of its inverse function ${\\color{red}f^{-1}(x)}.$\n\n- The range of a function ${\\color{blue}f(x)}$ is the domain of its inverse function ${\\color{red}f^{-1}(x)}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179882 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179656 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5828ff13-ff61-55c4-8b7d-7147118b03b0" "question-a049f227-9a74-52c4-a177-20bed5af6f28"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179511 :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{x} - 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nSince we have $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}{\\color{red}\\,-\\,2},$ we need to translate the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ *down* by $\\color{red}2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of $y=\\dfrac 1 x$ is translated down by $2$ units, the horizontal asymptote $y=0$ is also translated down by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new horizontal asymptote is $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/b1/b157913f6fca24190330cdfa5c795e069eff15de8a2079d9e29e1d6e41b95cb4.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179657 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cdddff3e-12b7-51e5-bbd8-54acd953d28d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179512 :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nSince we have $y = \\dfrac{1}{x \\color{red} + 2},$ we need to translate the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ to the *left* by $\\color{red}2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 x$ is translated to the left by $2$ units, the vertical asymptote $x = 0$ is also translated to the left by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new vertical asymptote is $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/e8/e8bff71235e67d8ad4046e94b5fe2682543674877a253ae6d4fe431504dbde3c.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179658 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8042fdd6-a329-5d96-ab3c-3d93ab337f8f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179513 :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-1}+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The given function can be obtained by translating $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ both horizontally and vertically.\n\nWe have two translations:\n\n- the $\\color{red}-1$ in the denominator means a horizontal translation of $1$ unit to the *right*, and\n\n- the $\\color{blue}+2$ outside the fraction means a vertical translation of $2$ units *up*.\n\nSo, the new graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fe/fea5a705657b22621f3eb5ffe40cfcd59aef11036674d47e6d1f72e301d531f3.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-5828ff13-ff61-55c4-8b7d-7147118b03b0" :question/id #uuid "5828ff13-ff61-55c4-8b7d-7147118b03b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74372" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}-2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/1c/1cc1a1f5fa2bf98d700bf0c8d8e0630a4fda0540b8715865ee72d34b17049b8a.png)\n\nSince we have $y=-\\frac{1}{x}\\,-\\,2,$ we need to translate the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *down* by $2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is translated down by $2$ units, the horizontal asymptote $y=0$ is also translated down by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new horizontal asymptote is $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/10/10c52c6f400ff5880358c6653f2cd0341aed312317d88438a545b2522e19b61b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ac7ab396-9bb4-5f64-be8c-3f934bdab4d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ac7ab396-9bb4-5f64-be8c-3f934bdab4d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac7ab396-9bb4-5f64-be8c-3f934bdab4d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9b71c6d8-23d9-5cfc-a5fb-af31bdaa4b87" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9b71c6d8-23d9-5cfc-a5fb-af31bdaa4b87" "answer-4b3f159b-1541-5e92-952b-f1551c9af726" "answer-a7b42cb5-47d2-5026-b8e6-267265a4d840" "answer-3375677f-2605-5437-aded-b6c2230d05ae" "answer-4c223c57-5e79-59f7-82f7-d42e3d82bde2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b71c6d8-23d9-5cfc-a5fb-af31bdaa4b87" :answer/id #uuid "9b71c6d8-23d9-5cfc-a5fb-af31bdaa4b87" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/10/10c52c6f400ff5880358c6653f2cd0341aed312317d88438a545b2522e19b61b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b3f159b-1541-5e92-952b-f1551c9af726" :answer/id #uuid "4b3f159b-1541-5e92-952b-f1551c9af726" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/56/56d343e13ae9af0bd1f204f82f11abc685aa0b33f627b8d1ad175be34814fba0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7b42cb5-47d2-5026-b8e6-267265a4d840" :answer/id #uuid "a7b42cb5-47d2-5026-b8e6-267265a4d840" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/66/662f8c5ea9d851e9ba0b0fa5343a0dd0831b3d22745b24e1db138caadfcc51d2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3375677f-2605-5437-aded-b6c2230d05ae" :answer/id #uuid "3375677f-2605-5437-aded-b6c2230d05ae" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ac/ac83d9afe3a7c3333f9db674eee4458cfe5f210d58b5c48a5397b83be5fe103c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c223c57-5e79-59f7-82f7-d42e3d82bde2" :answer/id #uuid "4c223c57-5e79-59f7-82f7-d42e3d82bde2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/fa/fa40fe0970b99770dfa6f228f4ed7b49db74d03c37162879b3458d83d340e7c1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a049f227-9a74-52c4-a177-20bed5af6f28" :question/id #uuid "a049f227-9a74-52c4-a177-20bed5af6f28" :question/math-academy-id "q-74370" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}+2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/1c/1cc1a1f5fa2bf98d700bf0c8d8e0630a4fda0540b8715865ee72d34b17049b8a.png)\n\nSince we have $y=-\\frac{1}{x}\\,+\\,2,$ we need to translate the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *up* by $2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is translated up by $2$ units, the horizontal asymptote $y=0$ is also translated up by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/fa/fa40fe0970b99770dfa6f228f4ed7b49db74d03c37162879b3458d83d340e7c1.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73b8b396-f960-5434-9408-2683ac668e9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73b8b396-f960-5434-9408-2683ac668e9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "73b8b396-f960-5434-9408-2683ac668e9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-90cffb8a-d87b-5902-a447-970ea110de92" :answer-field/choices ["answer-de0532c3-9ba6-579a-b169-217d96152121" "answer-f92d9fab-b888-55fb-a357-3b881cd30b6a" "answer-54c0b57c-a29e-5dd5-aff4-839755f093bd" "answer-90cffb8a-d87b-5902-a447-970ea110de92" "answer-0ff81a49-0d16-5746-903d-60f6f455bb68"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de0532c3-9ba6-579a-b169-217d96152121" :answer/id #uuid "de0532c3-9ba6-579a-b169-217d96152121" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/56/56d343e13ae9af0bd1f204f82f11abc685aa0b33f627b8d1ad175be34814fba0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f92d9fab-b888-55fb-a357-3b881cd30b6a" :answer/id #uuid "f92d9fab-b888-55fb-a357-3b881cd30b6a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b8/b8ddc086b4a2a6e9f29843db6a861357271f14c138201ea314e6653ab51d1113.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54c0b57c-a29e-5dd5-aff4-839755f093bd" :answer/id #uuid "54c0b57c-a29e-5dd5-aff4-839755f093bd" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ca/ca3d18d029