-d24d-50fd-80d6-30c09ae04436" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6d1762ad-5abf-509d-8ce1-df3d1de97be1" "answer-f1ba747c-3f06-595b-88ee-b1df82b6a1d3" "answer-41312684-7225-5ecc-8e04-52bdce722210" "answer-9d6db429-81a1-5bdc-a59a-56dfc564feb4" "answer-09f325bf-d24d-50fd-80d6-30c09ae04436"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d1762ad-5abf-509d-8ce1-df3d1de97be1" :answer/id #uuid "6d1762ad-5abf-509d-8ce1-df3d1de97be1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{10\\},\\{20\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1ba747c-3f06-595b-88ee-b1df82b6a1d3" :answer/id #uuid "f1ba747c-3f06-595b-88ee-b1df82b6a1d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{10\\},\\{20\\},\\{10,20\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41312684-7225-5ecc-8e04-52bdce722210" :answer/id #uuid "41312684-7225-5ecc-8e04-52bdce722210" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∅,\\{10,20\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d6db429-81a1-5bdc-a59a-56dfc564feb4" :answer/id #uuid "9d6db429-81a1-5bdc-a59a-56dfc564feb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∅,\\{10\\},\\{20\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09f325bf-d24d-50fd-80d6-30c09ae04436" :answer/id #uuid "09f325bf-d24d-50fd-80d6-30c09ae04436" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∅,\\{10\\},\\{20\\},\\{10,20\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-68f004d9-140c-5382-893d-19e68749e384" :question/id #uuid "68f004d9-140c-5382-893d-19e68749e384" :question/math-academy-id "q-105037" :question/problem "Which statement is **not** true?" :question/worked-solution "If $A⊆B,$ then $A$ is a subset of $B,$ meaning that every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nAmong the given choices, only $R⊆Q$ is not true. This statement is not true because not every real number is a rational number. For example, we have $\\sqrt{2}\\in R$ but $\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2bad22a0-e25f-5db1-9827-75aee3ba7896"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2bad22a0-e25f-5db1-9827-75aee3ba7896" :answer-field/id #uuid "2bad22a0-e25f-5db1-9827-75aee3ba7896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4809c7b0-7a53-5da1-8ec2-403d36b43401" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0fc727f2-c118-59d9-bef1-319f61ac67af" "answer-b931012e-07aa-53b1-8019-98f569760f38" "answer-4809c7b0-7a53-5da1-8ec2-403d36b43401" "answer-6d297646-3077-5fe0-8d3c-2ea9298474ab" "answer-bfe47b9d-e35e-5bce-9bca-5d9133925e90"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fc727f2-c118-59d9-bef1-319f61ac67af" :answer/id #uuid "0fc727f2-c118-59d9-bef1-319f61ac67af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N⊆R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b931012e-07aa-53b1-8019-98f569760f38" :answer/id #uuid "b931012e-07aa-53b1-8019-98f569760f38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R⊆C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4809c7b0-7a53-5da1-8ec2-403d36b43401" :answer/id #uuid "4809c7b0-7a53-5da1-8ec2-403d36b43401" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R⊆Q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d297646-3077-5fe0-8d3c-2ea9298474ab" :answer/id #uuid "6d297646-3077-5fe0-8d3c-2ea9298474ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{Z}^{+}⊆R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfe47b9d-e35e-5bce-9bca-5d9133925e90" :answer/id #uuid "bfe47b9d-e35e-5bce-9bca-5d9133925e90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{Z}^{-}⊆Z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055352 :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ (the set of non-negative rational numbers)?\n\nI. $Z$\n\nII. ${Z}^{+}$ (the set of positive integers)\n\nIII. $N$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $Z$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{+}.$ For example, $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{+}.$\n\n- ${Z}^{+}$ is  a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{+}.$ Every positive integer is a positive rational number.\n\n- $N$ a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{+}.$ Every natural number is a positive rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186053349 :answer-field/correct 17592186053352 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-bbde3d52-89b7-557b-8e4a-7c74e15e7a66" :question/id #uuid "bbde3d52-89b7-557b-8e4a-7c74e15e7a66" :question/math-academy-id "q-1207" :question/problem "Which statement is **not** true?" :question/worked-solution "If $A⊆B,$ then $A$ is a subset of $B,$ meaning that every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nAmong the given choices, only $Z⊆N$ is not true. This statement is not true because not every integer is a natural number. For example, we have $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin N.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8ce1692e-cf75-5e60-a6af-ad45e3d2af68"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8ce1692e-cf75-5e60-a6af-ad45e3d2af68" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ce1692e-cf75-5e60-a6af-ad45e3d2af68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3b3bbb80-6390-5a44-9e0f-209ae6d10d67" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b1d0871-97c5-5ceb-ad1e-164735f98f5f" "answer-d3ead724-b0e7-5847-9028-7588d490d1fe" "answer-3b3bbb80-6390-5a44-9e0f-209ae6d10d67" "answer-c71b5cfb-d772-5c93-9c01-9dd436e02e4e" "answer-20475b75-f56f-5575-b976-5acb98cbf337"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b1d0871-97c5-5ceb-ad1e-164735f98f5f" :answer/id #uuid "2b1d0871-97c5-5ceb-ad1e-164735f98f5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N⊆Z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3ead724-b0e7-5847-9028-7588d490d1fe" :answer/id #uuid "d3ead724-b0e7-5847-9028-7588d490d1fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q⊆R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b3bbb80-6390-5a44-9e0f-209ae6d10d67" :answer/id #uuid "3b3bbb80-6390-5a44-9e0f-209ae6d10d67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Z⊆N" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c71b5cfb-d772-5c93-9c01-9dd436e02e4e" :answer/id #uuid "c71b5cfb-d772-5c93-9c01-9dd436e02e4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Z⊆Q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20475b75-f56f-5575-b976-5acb98cbf337" :answer/id #uuid "20475b75-f56f-5575-b976-5acb98cbf337" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Z⊆Z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-83ab9b35-c1e3-561b-9f67-abfc0e8e8a12" :question/id #uuid "83ab9b35-c1e3-561b-9f67-abfc0e8e8a12" :question/math-academy-id "q-80841" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A=\\{x\\in Z∣-1\\le x+2\\le 3\\}$ and $B=\\{x\\in Z∣{x}^{2}<2\\}?$\n\nI. $A⊆B$\n\nII. $B⊆A$\n\nIII. $B⊈A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $-1\\le x+2\\le 3,$ that is to say, $-3\\le x\\le 1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{-3,-2,-1,0,1\\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}<2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=\\{-1,0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $-3\\in A$ but $-3\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Indeed, every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b7fc5eab-4c18-5a60-8fc6-c5a0861c2c1b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b7fc5eab-4c18-5a60-8fc6-c5a0861c2c1b" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7fc5eab-4c18-5a60-8fc6-c5a0861c2c1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-40a33e13-388a-525b-83c0-b7ed6105ead4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c7b00fd0-7905-5e65-91ef-6e0c3c8ffeca" "answer-3c91d41d-35ad-5cb1-8da6-37cb13223ce2" "answer-5b1b09ec-5afa-5e99-aefe-b392810acdae" "answer-40a33e13-388a-525b-83c0-b7ed6105ead4" "answer-2d638923-cecf-59a3-9dd7-95b010b744a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7b00fd0-7905-5e65-91ef-6e0c3c8ffeca" :answer/id #uuid "c7b00fd0-7905-5e65-91ef-6e0c3c8ffeca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c91d41d-35ad-5cb1-8da6-37cb13223ce2" :answer/id #uuid "3c91d41d-35ad-5cb1-8da6-37cb13223ce2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b1b09ec-5afa-5e99-aefe-b392810acdae" :answer/id #uuid "5b1b09ec-5afa-5e99-aefe-b392810acdae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40a33e13-388a-525b-83c0-b7ed6105ead4" :answer/id #uuid "40a33e13-388a-525b-83c0-b7ed6105ead4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d638923-cecf-59a3-9dd7-95b010b744a1" :answer/id #uuid "2d638923-cecf-59a3-9dd7-95b010b744a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca4e5acb-c615-5b3e-863b-498834fef823" :question/id #uuid "ca4e5acb-c615-5b3e-863b-498834fef823" :question/math-academy-id "q-37262" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A=\\{n\\in N∣n+1\\ge 2\\}$ and $B=\\{n\\in Z∣n-1\\ge -2\\}?$\n\nI. $A⊆B$\n\nII. $∅⊈A$\n\nIII. $B⊈A$\n\nIV. $B⊇A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $n+1\\ge 2,$ that is to say, $n\\ge 1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6,…\\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n-1\\ge -2,$ That is to say, $n\\ge -1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=\\{-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,…\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $∅⊈A$ is false. The null set $∅=\\{\\}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊈A$ is true. For example, $0\\in B$ but $0\\notin A.$\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d3b7eed1-707d-5cdf-8d4a-2b44d5dd6b20"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d3b7eed1-707d-5cdf-8d4a-2b44d5dd6b20" :answer-field/id #uuid "d3b7eed1-707d-5cdf-8d4a-2b44d5dd6b20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3346d54c-0156-5635-a6e1-8679f717dab0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-69a3d84c-451e-565a-a9c3-67039d27f8ba" "answer-45a3acd8-8fac-5480-9456-949aed4deb1a" "answer-69e94d24-ed64-50bd-9e61-d489a3365853" "answer-3346d54c-0156-5635-a6e1-8679f717dab0" "answer-0c888f0e-54ba-51cb-a28f-bb550f2ccfe7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69a3d84c-451e-565a-a9c3-67039d27f8ba" :answer/id #uuid "69a3d84c-451e-565a-a9c3-67039d27f8ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45a3acd8-8fac-5480-9456-949aed4deb1a" :answer/id #uuid "45a3acd8-8fac-5480-9456-949aed4deb1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69e94d24-ed64-50bd-9e61-d489a3365853" :answer/id #uuid "69e94d24-ed64-50bd-9e61-d489a3365853" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3346d54c-0156-5635-a6e1-8679f717dab0" :answer/id #uuid "3346d54c-0156-5635-a6e1-8679f717dab0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c888f0e-54ba-51cb-a28f-bb550f2ccfe7" :answer/id #uuid "0c888f0e-54ba-51cb-a28f-bb550f2ccfe7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-713562a6-f85e-5713-a528-f52fb32799ee" :question/id #uuid "713562a6-f85e-5713-a528-f52fb32799ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-80843" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A=\\{x\\in N:x<5\\}$ and $B=\\{x\\in N:2x\\ge 4\\}?$\n\nI. $A⊆B$\n\nII. $B⊆A$\n\nIII. $∅⊆B$\n\nIV. $B⊇A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all natural numbers less than $5.$ So,\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers which satisfy the inequality $2x\\ge 4,$ that is to say $x\\ge 2.$ So,\n\n$$\nB=\\{2,3,…\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $1\\in A$ but $1\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $5\\in B$ but $5\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅=\\{\\}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-84f2b8a0-f781-5fe2-ac9e-75c68e238f0f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-84f2b8a0-f781-5fe2-ac9e-75c68e238f0f" :answer-field/id #uuid "84f2b8a0-f781-5fe2-ac9e-75c68e238f0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7c845076-ba15-5a9d-9053-a6b2e603927f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b5ca774-da1f-5977-a4df-c5f8f5210f15" "answer-b44e18f7-ca2a-59c7-9fb7-f38f7527a958" "answer-ff813adc-a63d-5297-b77f-e5f9fcf4f554" "answer-7691ae11-33ae-50a4-9eb9-de1fe4c7a7b6" "answer-7c845076-ba15-5a9d-9053-a6b2e603927f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b5ca774-da1f-5977-a4df-c5f8f5210f15" :answer/id #uuid "8b5ca774-da1f-5977-a4df-c5f8f5210f15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b44e18f7-ca2a-59c7-9fb7-f38f7527a958" :answer/id #uuid "b44e18f7-ca2a-59c7-9fb7-f38f7527a958" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff813adc-a63d-5297-b77f-e5f9fcf4f554" :answer/id #uuid "ff813adc-a63d-5297-b77f-e5f9fcf4f554" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7691ae11-33ae-50a4-9eb9-de1fe4c7a7b6" :answer/id #uuid "7691ae11-33ae-50a4-9eb9-de1fe4c7a7b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c845076-ba15-5a9d-9053-a6b2e603927f" :answer/id #uuid "7c845076-ba15-5a9d-9053-a6b2e603927f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056044 :tutorial/content "Given two sets $A$ and $B,$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we say that $A$ is a **subset** of $B,$ and we write \n\n$$\nA \\subseteq B.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ {\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{blue}{3}} \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1,2,3\\}, \\qquad C=\\{1,2,{\\color{red}4}\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that *every* element of $A$ is also an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}}\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3}}\\in B\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is a subset of $B,$ and we write $A \\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $A$ and $B$ as shown below. Notice that $A$ is contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/91/911dbbe9a638810f3a245fdcd004e10aee4dbd49fd8a99741c1bdd5c48cc2319.png)\n\nOn the other hand, not every element of $C$ is an element of $B{:}$ \n\n$$\n1 \\in B, \\qquad 2\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{red}4}\\notin B\n$$\n\nSince $4 \\notin B,$ we have that $C$ is *not* a subset of $B,$ and we write $C \\not\\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $B$ and $C$ as follows. Notice that $C$ is *not* contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/45/454c28bacb8d5117bb11f76988b70344b4521d260adf2b690a11e7dfd2964385.png)\n\nAnother way of thinking about the statement $C \\not\\subseteq B$ is that $C$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $B.$ In this case, that element is $4.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056056 :tutorial/content "Recall that the natural numbers, integers, rational numbers, and real and complex numbers can be arranged in the following hierarchical diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66260e4c239137cd30453d9335256fd7d84e93a197612d670c19d97e417bd2f0.png)\n\nIn other words, $\\mathbb{N}$ is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Z},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Q},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{R},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{C}.$ We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R} \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}\n$$\n\nSince none of these sets are equivalent, we can also write\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z} \\subset \\mathbb{Q} \\subset \\mathbb{R} \\subset \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nSome restricted or extended special sets are subsets of others. For example:\n\n- $\\mathbb N$ is the set of natural numbers, and $\\mathbb N_0$ is the set of natural numbers *and* zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\mathbb N\\subset \\mathbb N_0.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Z$ is the set of integers, $\\mathbb Z^+$ is the set of positive integers (equivalent to $\\mathbb N$), and $\\mathbb Z^+_0$ is the set of positive integers and zero (equivalent to $\\mathbb N_0$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z^+\\subset  \\mathbb Z_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Q^+$ is the set of positive rational numbers, $\\mathbb R^+$ is the set of positive real numbers, and $\\mathbb R$ is the set of real numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Q^+\\subset  \\mathbb R^+ \\subset \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nHowever, note that, for example, $\\mathbb Q \\not\\subset  \\mathbb R^+,$ since $0\\in\\mathbb Q$ yet $0\\notin \\mathbb R^+.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056075 :tutorial/content "You might wonder why we need to define a universal set. Can't we just let the universal set be the set of everything?\n\nThe answer is no. If we don't specify a well-defined universal set, we encounter a nonsensical situation called **Russell's paradox.**\n\nFirst, suppose there exists a set $\\Omega$ that contains absolutely everything, including every mathematical object, every thought, every book, every person, animal, cell, and star in the universe, past, present, or future. \n\nRussell's paradox originates from the following idea:\n\n*Define $S$ as the set that contains all sets that are not elements of themselves.* \n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\in \\Omega\\,:\\, A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nMost sets you can think of are elements of $S.$ For example, the set $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is in $S$ because $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}\\notin \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$ \n\nIt takes a bit of thought to find sets that *aren't* members of $S.$ Such sets do exist, although typical examples are somewhat abstract: \n\n- The set containing all possible sets.\n\n- The set containing all possible thoughts.\n\n- The set $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = \\Big\\{\\underbrace{\\big\\{ \\left\\{\\{\\ldots \\}\\right\\}\\big\\}}_{X}\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nHowever, what about $S$ itself? Is $S$ an element of $S?$ There are only two possibilities:\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction because we cannot have $S\\in S$ *and* $S\\notin S.$\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we must have that $S\\in S.$ But this leads to the same contradiction as before!\n\nThe only way to resolve this is to conclude that $S\\notin \\Omega,$ but this is also a contradiction since $\\Omega$ contains absolutely everything!  So, there is no consistent answer to the question of whether $S$ itself is an element of $S.$ This is Russell's paradox, and when it was discovered, it shook the foundations of mathematics to its very core.\n\nLater, it was realized that if a set is defined using a predicate, we should ensure that all sets involved are subsets of some larger set $U$ (i.e., a universal set) where we can always determine which elements are members of $U$ and which are not, at least in principle.\n\nTo see how this resolves Russell's paradox, suppose $U$ is a universal set. Let's refine our definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\subseteq U\\,:\\,A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume $S$ is a set, so $S\\subseteq U$ for some well-defined $U.$ Once again, we'll ask the question of whether $S\\in S{:}$\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction.\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\in S.$ But this is also a contradiction.\n\nThus, to resolve the paradox, we're left with no choice but to conclude that $S\\not\\subseteq U.$ This indicates that something is fundamentally wrong with our definition of $S,$ and no such set can exist because if it were a set, it would belong to a universal set." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054909 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
